<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        The iOS side carries NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription, so the app
        asks for location somewhere. COARSE only: nothing here needs a street
        address, and FINE triggers a second, harsher permission dialog plus a
        Play Console declaration.
    -->
    <!-- ====================================================================
         LOCATION IS DECLARED NOWHERE, BECAUSE IT IS COLLECTED NOWHERE
         ====================================================================
         ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION was declared here and the only code that ever
         used it is `features/life/hooks/useWeatherLogic.ts`, reached from
         `WeatherScreen`, inside the `life` module - which has been
         `life: false` in core/featureFlags.ts for a long time.

         So the app asked for a permission it could not use. That is not a
         harmless leftover:

           - Play Console's Data Safety form has to agree with the manifest,
             and a declared permission with no matching disclosure is a
             mismatch Google rejects for.
           - Apple's privacy label would be wrong whichever way it was filled
             in, and a reviewer who reads "location" and finds no prompt asks
             why.
           - A privacy policy that says location is not collected is
             contradicted by the manifest, and one that says it is would be a
             lie about an app that never asks.

         SHELVED RATHER THAN DELETED, like the code it belongs to. Turning
         `life` back on means putting this line back, and
         androidPermissions.test.ts fails until somebody does, so the pair
         cannot drift apart again.

         <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />
         ==================================================================== -->

    <!--
        THE ADVERTISING ID, AND WHY IT HAS TO BE DECLARED

        From Android 13 the advertising ID is a permission rather than
        something every app simply has. Google Play Services does not fail
        loudly when it is missing: the ad SDK carries on, requests a
        non-personalised ad instead, and fills at a much lower rate. So the
        symptom is not a crash or a log line, it is revenue that is quietly a
        fraction of what it should be, which is unfalsifiable from the sofa.

        Declaring it also obliges a Data Safety answer in the Play Console.
        That is a form to fill in, not a reason to leave the permission out.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="com.google.android.gms.permission.AD_ID" />

    <application
      android:name=".MainApplication"
      android:label="@string/app_name"
      android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
      android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
      android:allowBackup="false"
      android:theme="@style/AppTheme"
      android:usesCleartextTraffic="${usesCleartextTraffic}"
      android:supportsRtl="true">

      <!--
          THE ADMOB APPLICATION ID IS NOT WRITTEN HERE. IT COMES FROM app.json.

          SHELVED, and kept with its reasoning rather than removed, because
          this is what every AdMob guide tells you to do and the next person
          to read those guides will be me.

              <meta-data
                android:name="com.google.android.gms.ads.APPLICATION_ID"
                android:value="ca-app-pub-9881104469619624~7205239960" />

          That is correct for the bare Google SDK and WRONG for this library.
          react-native-google-mobile-ads declares the very same meta-data name
          in its own manifest, with the value as a placeholder it fills from
          the "react-native-google-mobile-ads" key in app.json. Two manifests
          declaring one meta-data name with two different values is a merger
          conflict, and the build fails asking for tools:replace.

          Worse if it had not conflicted: the library ALSO feeds the iOS
          Info.plist from that same app.json block, through a CocoaPods build
          script. So a hand-written value here would have been one of two
          sources of truth, and the other one is the one that ships.

          One place now: app.json. src/core/ads/config.test.ts checks that it
          agrees with config.ts.
      -->

      <activity
        android:name=".MainActivity"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:configChanges="keyboard|keyboardHidden|orientation|screenLayout|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|uiMode"
        android:launchMode="singleTask"
        android:screenOrientation="portrait"
        android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
        android:theme="@style/BootTheme"
        android:exported="true">
        <intent-filter>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent-filter>
      </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
